1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:08,192
This is ECE 320, video 11. The topic is branches in the pipeline, and branch prediction.

2
00:00:08,642 --> 00:00:23,319
After this video, you can draw a pipeline diagram with a branch and a predictor. You can calculate the branch CPI. And you can fill predictor tables, as in assignment 4 and the spring 2020 quiz.

3
00:00:23,769 --> 00:00:36,548
F must fetch a new instruction in every cycle, so it needs the next P C. A branch decides it. Taken means the next P C is the target. Not taken means P C plus 4.

4
00:00:36,998 --> 00:00:47,387
F knows only the P C. D knows the opcode and the offset, so it knows the target. Only X knows the outcome, and the jump register value.

5
00:00:47,837 --> 00:00:58,248
So the next P C is decided in X, but F needs it earlier. By then, F and D hold two younger instructions. This is a control hazard.

6
00:00:58,698 --> 00:01:07,210
Option a: stall F until the branch is in X. Orange marks the branch in X. Every branch loses 2 cycles.

7
00:01:07,660 --> 00:01:19,052
Option b: guess and keep fetching. At fetch, the hardware cannot tell that it is a branch. So the default guess is not taken. If that is right, no cycle is lost.

8
00:01:19,502 --> 00:01:27,651
Option c: the branch is taken, so the guess is wrong. The two wrong instructions are removed. 2 cycles are lost.

9
00:01:28,101 --> 00:01:39,899
This is speculation. The game is percent correct times gain, minus percent wrong times penalty. A wrong guess costs no more than waiting, so guessing never loses.

10
00:01:40,349 --> 00:01:48,733
Here is slide 4, drawn with the course rules. The guess is not taken. In cycles 1 to 3, F fetches in order.

11
00:01:49,183 --> 00:02:00,916
In cycle 4, the branch is in X, and it is taken. The green arrow is M X forwarding of x 3. Store word in D, and add in F, are on the wrong path.

12
00:02:01,366 --> 00:02:10,369
In cycle 5, both become nops, shown as dashes. F fetches the target in the same cycle. The penalty is 2 cycles.

13
00:02:10,819 --> 00:02:13,165
The target finishes in cycle 9.

14
00:02:13,615 --> 00:02:25,349
The flush is safe. Registers change only in W, and memory only in M. The wrong instructions reached only D, so they changed no permanent state.

15
00:02:25,799 --> 00:02:37,489
Slide 6 shows the hardware, simplified here. F has the P C and the instruction memory. X has the branch compare unit, and an adder for P C plus immediate.

16
00:02:37,939 --> 00:02:47,241
Two signals meet in the control. Branch comes from decode. Cond comes from the branch compare unit. Taken means Branch and Cond.

17
00:02:47,691 --> 00:02:56,736
If taken, P C Sel selects the target. F Kill and D Kill set the kill bits, so the two younger instructions become nops.

18
00:02:57,186 --> 00:03:06,381
Slide 7: a conditional branch. Its condition is known in X. If it is taken, the two instructions behind it become bubbles.

19
00:03:06,831 --> 00:03:16,879
Slide 8: jump and link register. Its target is a register value, and that value is sure only in X. So it has the same two bubbles.

20
00:03:17,329 --> 00:03:28,507
With the guess not taken, every taken branch loses 2 cycles. Every J A L R also loses 2 cycles, because it always changes the P C.

21
00:03:28,957 --> 00:03:35,123
Slide 9 gives a quick estimate. 20 percent of instructions are conditional branches.

22
00:03:35,573 --> 00:03:42,357
75 percent of them are taken. With the guess not taken, each taken branch is a wrong guess.

23
00:03:42,807 --> 00:03:52,151
Each wrong guess costs 2 cycles. So the CPI is 1, plus point 2 times point 7 5 times 2. That is 1.3.

24
00:03:52,601 --> 00:04:00,558
So branches make the program 30 percent slower. A deeper pipeline has a larger penalty, so the loss is worse.

25
00:04:01,008 --> 00:04:10,096
To do better, the hardware must guess the outcome. The guess must be made in F. But the instruction bits are ready only at the end of F.

26
00:04:10,546 --> 00:04:23,005
So the predictor uses only the P C. It has two parts. A target buffer maps a P C to its taken target. A direction predictor maps a P C to taken or not taken.

27
00:04:23,455 --> 00:04:37,449
Slide 18 asks three questions: is it a branch, is it taken, and where does it go. In F, the predictor answers all three. The branch still resolves in X, and a wrong guess is flushed, as before.

28
00:04:37,899 --> 00:04:49,270
To map a P C, use some P C bits as an index into an array. N entries need log 2 of N bits. So 256 entries need 8 bits.

29
00:04:49,720 --> 00:05:01,837
Use the lowest bits after bits 1 and 0. Those two are always zero, because instructions are 4 bytes. The low bits change most often, so they spread the branches best.

30
00:05:02,287 --> 00:05:17,519
Hex 1048 and hex 1448 differ only in bit 10. Both use entry hex 12. This is aliasing. It is OK, because the data is only a guess, and X still checks.

31
00:05:17,969 --> 00:05:33,329
The simplest direction predictor is the branch history table, B H T. Most branches are biased, for example loop back edges. Each entry is one bit: 0 for not taken, 1 for taken. There are no tags.

32
00:05:33,779 --> 00:05:38,942
In F, the bit is the prediction: the branch goes the same way as last time.

33
00:05:39,392 --> 00:05:47,051
In X, the real outcome is known. Through a write port, the predictor writes a new value only if the guess was wrong.

34
00:05:47,501 --> 00:05:59,597
Slide 21 tests the B H T on an inner loop with 3 passes. Its branch goes taken, taken, taken, not taken, again and again. The entry starts at N.

35
00:06:00,047 --> 00:06:09,412
Time 1 is wrong, and the entry becomes T. Times 2 and 3 are correct. Time 4 is the exit: wrong, and the entry becomes N.

36
00:06:09,862 --> 00:06:15,942
Time 5 starts the next run. The entry says N, but the branch is taken. Wrong again.

37
00:06:16,392 --> 00:06:19,421
The pattern repeats: 6 misses in 12.

38
00:06:19,871 --> 00:06:28,042
So there are two built-in misses per run: the exit, and the next first pass. The predictor changes its mind too quickly.

39
00:06:28,492 --> 00:06:44,087
The fix is the two-bit saturating counter. It counts from 0 to 3: N, small n, small t, and T. Taken adds 1, not taken subtracts 1, and it stops at 0 and 3. t and T predict taken.

40
00:06:44,537 --> 00:06:50,510
Same branch. Time 1: state N predicts not taken. It is taken: wrong. Move to n.

41
00:06:50,960 --> 00:06:54,693
Time 2: n predicts not taken. Wrong again. Move to t.

42
00:06:55,143 --> 00:06:58,685
Time 3: t predicts taken. Correct. Move to T.

43
00:06:59,135 --> 00:07:07,071
Time 4 is the exit. T predicts taken: wrong. But the counter moves only to t, which still predicts taken.

44
00:07:07,521 --> 00:07:15,862
Time 5: t predicts taken. Correct. The counter must miss twice before it changes its mind. This is hysteresis.

45
00:07:16,312 --> 00:07:21,752
In total, 5 misses. After warm-up, only one miss per run, at the exit.

46
00:07:22,202 --> 00:07:31,098
Branches can be correlated. In this code from slide 24, if conditions A and B are true, condition C is false.

47
00:07:31,548 --> 00:07:39,484
A branch history shift register, the B H R, holds the recent outcomes. A new outcome enters at the right.

48
00:07:39,934 --> 00:07:48,275
The P C selects a row, and the B H R selects a column. So each branch has one prediction per history pattern.

49
00:07:48,725 --> 00:08:00,928
Slide 26 uses the same T T T N branch with 2 history bits. So it has 4 one-bit entries. All start at N, and the B H R starts at N N.

50
00:08:01,378 --> 00:08:15,373
Time 1 reads entry N N, marked orange. It is wrong, so N N becomes T. The outcome T shifts in, and the B H R becomes N T. Time 2 reads N T, and is also wrong.

51
00:08:15,823 --> 00:08:26,255
Time 3 reads T T: wrong, so T T becomes T. Time 4 also sees T T, but this is the exit. Wrong, and T T becomes N.

52
00:08:26,705 --> 00:08:30,161
In times 5 to 8, only time 6 is correct.

53
00:08:30,611 --> 00:08:41,171
In total, 9 misses: worse than the 2-bit counter. History T T comes before both T and N. The history is too short to capture the pattern.

54
00:08:41,621 --> 00:08:57,791
Slide 27 uses 3 history bits, so 8 entries. In times 1 to 6, each new pattern starts at N. So the predictor misses 5 times while it learns. Time 4 is correct only because the exit is N.

55
00:08:58,241 --> 00:09:04,279
From time 7, every prediction is correct. Each pattern of the loop has its own entry.

56
00:09:04,729 --> 00:09:14,243
History T T T is always followed by the exit. N T T is followed by taken. The history must be long enough to see the pattern.

57
00:09:14,693 --> 00:09:28,048
A hybrid predictor combines two schemes. A simple B H T predicts independent branches. A correlated B H T, with global history, predicts the branches that need history.

58
00:09:28,498 --> 00:09:40,359
A chooser assigns each branch to one of them. Branches start in the simple B H T, and move after too many misses. Accuracy is 90 to 97 percent.

59
00:09:40,809 --> 00:09:52,073
When should we predict? In D, the target is known. But F has already fetched P C plus 4. So even a correct taken guess costs one cycle: a mis-fetch.

60
00:09:52,523 --> 00:10:00,651
In F, a correct guess costs 0 cycles. But F knows only the P C. So it needs the branch target buffer.

61
00:10:01,101 --> 00:10:14,221
The branch target buffer, B T B, records past targets. If branch X went to Y last time, fetch Y after X. It is read in F, in parallel with the instruction memory.

62
00:10:14,671 --> 00:10:27,002
Every instruction reads the B T B. Each entry has a tag, the full P C. A tag match means a known branch: use its target. Otherwise use P C plus 4.

63
00:10:27,452 --> 00:10:35,367
Every taken branch writes its entry in X. The tag gets the P C, and the target gets the target P C.

64
00:10:35,817 --> 00:10:51,454
An example. The index is P C bits 9 to 2. Hex 4 1 C and hex 1 C both give entry 7. But the tag there is hex 1 C. No match, so fetch P C plus 4. The tag stops aliasing.

65
00:10:51,904 --> 00:11:00,800
A B T B works because most branches use direct targets. The target is in the instruction, so it is the same every time.

66
00:11:01,250 --> 00:11:13,303
Indirect targets are in a register, so they can change. J A L R is common, because every procedure return is one. Each caller has a different return address.

67
00:11:13,753 --> 00:11:23,887
The return address stack, R A S, predicts returns. A call pushes P C plus 4. A return pops the top as its predicted target.

68
00:11:24,337 --> 00:11:34,385
Call 1 pushes hex 104. Return 1 pops hex 104: correct. The B T B guesses P C plus 4: wrong.

69
00:11:34,835 --> 00:11:46,248
Call 2 pushes hex 304. Return 2 pops hex 304: correct. The B T B gives the old target, hex 104: wrong.

70
00:11:46,698 --> 00:11:57,237
F must know before decode that it is a return, because a pop changes the stack. Pre-decode bits in the instruction memory mark calls and returns.

71
00:11:57,687 --> 00:12:07,628
Slide 35 puts it all together. Start with the base. In F, the P C goes to the plus 4 adder, and the mux gives the next P C.

72
00:12:08,078 --> 00:12:15,289
In parallel, the P C reads the B T B, marked orange. On a tag hit, it gives the stored target.

73
00:12:15,739 --> 00:12:21,029
The P C also reads the B H T. It predicts taken or not taken.

74
00:12:21,479 --> 00:12:29,885
The R A S gives the return P C. The pre-decode bits in the instruction memory tell if the instruction is a return.

75
00:12:30,335 --> 00:12:40,831
The mux picks the R A S top for a return. It picks the B T B target on a tag hit with a taken prediction. Otherwise, P C plus 4.

76
00:12:41,281 --> 00:12:57,601
A correct guess costs 0 cycles, even when taken. The CPI is 1.1 with 75 percent accuracy, and 1.02 with 95 percent. Real penalties are often 10 cycles or more.

77
00:12:58,051 --> 00:13:09,187
Predication removes the branch. In conventional code, a mis-predicted branch flushes both sub and add. But add is on both paths, so flushing it is waste.

78
00:13:09,637 --> 00:13:20,218
With predication, every instruction is fetched. A predicate register, p 3, controls sub. If it is false, sub is annulled in place, and add is kept.

79
00:13:20,668 --> 00:13:31,612
The benefit: no mis-predictions, and fewer branches in the tables. The cost: annulled instructions still use slots. With accurate predictors, it may not help.

80
00:13:32,062 --> 00:13:43,582
A practice problem with the slide numbers. Pause the video and try it. CPI equals 1, plus the branch fraction, times the wrong fraction, times the penalty.

81
00:13:44,032 --> 00:13:49,664
a. Stall on every branch: 1 plus point 2 times 1 times 2 is 1.4.

82
00:13:50,114 --> 00:13:57,026
b. Predict not taken: point 7 5 of the branches are wrong. The CPI is 1.3.

83
00:13:57,476 --> 00:14:03,919
c. 75 percent correct means 25 percent wrong. The CPI is 1.1.

84
00:14:04,369 --> 00:14:10,875
d. 95 percent correct means 5 percent wrong. The CPI is 1.02.

85
00:14:11,325 --> 00:14:19,752
e. With a 10-cycle penalty, the CPI is 1.1 again. A good predictor matters more in a deep pipeline.

86
00:14:20,202 --> 00:14:35,327
Assignment 4. Pause and try each part. This 2-bit machine is not the slide counter. States 0 X predict taken. From 1 1, taken goes to 0 0. Always use the machine of the question.

87
00:14:35,777 --> 00:14:48,620
b1 skips the add when the element is 0. b2 loops while x1 is not 0. n is 8, the array alternates 1 and 0, and all entries start at 1 0.

88
00:14:49,070 --> 00:15:00,547
Part a. x2 counts the non-zero elements, so x2 is 4. b1 alternates not taken, taken. b2 is taken 7 times, then not taken.

89
00:15:00,997 --> 00:15:13,072
Part b, no history. Pause and try it. Yellow marks the entry read. Row 2: b2 reads 1 0 and predicts not taken, but it is taken. It moves to 1 1.

90
00:15:13,522 --> 00:15:24,786
Row 4 moved b2 from 1 1 to 0 0. So in row 6, b2 predicts taken: correct. In row 5, b1 reads 1 1 and is correct.

91
00:15:25,236 --> 00:15:35,412
b1 moves between 1 0 and 1 1. Both predict not taken, so every taken b1 misses. b2 stays at 0 0.

92
00:15:35,862 --> 00:15:43,521
The final b2 is not taken: a miss. Total: 7 mispredicts, 4 from b1 and 3 from b2.

93
00:15:43,971 --> 00:15:54,360
Part c adds one global history bit: the outcome of the last branch, b1 or b2. It starts at 1, and selects set 0 or set 1.

94
00:15:54,810 --> 00:16:03,514
Before each b1, the last branch is a taken b2. So b1 always reads set 1, the same as with no history.

95
00:16:03,964 --> 00:16:10,471
b2 follows b1, which alternates. So b2 trains both sets, with 2 misses each.

96
00:16:10,921 --> 00:16:18,771
With the final b2, that is 9 mispredicts: 4 from b1 and 5 from b2. One bit made it worse.

97
00:16:19,221 --> 00:16:26,688
Part d uses 2 history bits. Here the newest outcome enters on the left. The history starts at 1 1.

98
00:16:27,138 --> 00:16:35,245
b1 sees history 1 1 before a not taken, and 1 0 before a taken. So it uses two different entries.

99
00:16:35,695 --> 00:16:48,345
Entry S 1 0 trains to 0 0, which predicts taken. S 1 1 stays at 1 0, which predicts not taken. From row 9, b1 is always correct.

100
00:16:48,795 --> 00:16:56,582
Total: 7 mispredicts, 2 from b1 and 5 from b2. After warm-up, only the final b2 misses.

101
00:16:57,032 --> 00:17:02,856
Compare the results: 7, 9, and 7 mispredicts. All three miss the loop exit.

102
00:17:03,306 --> 00:17:14,506
Part e. More history bits mean more entries to train, so a longer start-up. One bit is too short-sighted to help b1. For large n, two bits are best.

103
00:17:14,956 --> 00:17:26,540
Part f. With random input, nothing predicts b1, but all learn b2. Part g. History helps only when there is a pattern, and the history is long enough to see it.

104
00:17:26,990 --> 00:17:41,625
Now the spring 2020 quiz, question 2 part A. There is no forwarding into D or into the branch compare unit. The branch is predicted taken, but resolves not taken. Pause and try it.

105
00:17:42,075 --> 00:17:53,253
Add needs t 0 from the load: a load-use stall in cycle 3, then W X forwarding in cycle 5. The branch is fetched in cycle 5.

106
00:17:53,703 --> 00:18:07,591
The branch needs a 0 from add immediate, with no forwarding. So it reads a 0 in D after that W, in cycle 9. The predictor says taken, so F fetches hex 10 from cycle 6.

107
00:18:08,041 --> 00:18:13,140
In cycle 10, the branch resolves in X: not taken. The guess was wrong.

108
00:18:13,590 --> 00:18:22,550
The wrong load and add become dashes. In cycle 11, F fetches the nop at hex 20. It finishes in cycle 15.

109
00:18:23,000 --> 00:18:36,803
But the quiz table has 14 columns. It fits if the register file writes, then reads, in one cycle. Then every later cell moves one cycle earlier. State your assumption in the exam.

110
00:18:37,253 --> 00:18:52,570
Part B adds a 2-bit counter B H T and a B T B. States 1 0 and 1 1 predict taken. a 0 starts at hex 1000, and a 1 is hex 1010. Pause and fill the table.

111
00:18:53,020 --> 00:19:05,287
At the branch, a 0 is hex 1004, 1008, then 100 C: taken. Then hex 1010: not taken. So the branch runs 4 times.

112
00:19:05,737 --> 00:19:15,145
Run 1 is given. 0 1 predicts not taken, but it is taken. Move to 1 0. The B T B gets the target hex 10.

113
00:19:15,595 --> 00:19:22,443
Runs 2 and 3: 1 0 and 1 1 predict taken, with target hex 10. Both are correct.

114
00:19:22,893 --> 00:19:32,173
Run 4: 1 1 predicts taken, but it is not taken. Move to 1 0. A not taken branch does not write the B T B.

115
00:19:32,623 --> 00:19:39,833
The last row: the entry is 1 0, predict taken, target hex 10. Rows 5 and 6 stay blank.

116
00:19:40,283 --> 00:19:48,475
Recap. One. Branches and J A L R resolve in X. By then, F and D hold two younger instructions.

117
00:19:48,925 --> 00:19:56,093
Two. A wrong guess flushes them, and fetches the right P C in the next cycle. The penalty is 2 cycles.

118
00:19:56,543 --> 00:20:03,029
Three. CPI is 1, plus the branch fraction, times the wrong fraction, times the penalty.

119
00:20:03,479 --> 00:20:10,519
Four. Index tables with the P C bits above bits 1 and 0. Aliasing only spoils a guess.

120
00:20:10,969 --> 00:20:18,819
Five. A one-bit B H T misses twice per loop run, a two-bit counter once. Use the machine of the question.

121
00:20:19,269 --> 00:20:25,840
Six. A history predictor keeps one entry per P C and pattern. The history must be long enough.

122
00:20:26,290 --> 00:20:34,055
Seven. In F, the B T B gives the target, the B H T the direction, and the R A S the return address.

123
00:20:34,505 --> 00:20:40,927
Eight. In the exam, state your register file assumption. That is the end of video 11.
